  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Instead of screaming at the mouse in her locker, Katya calmly uses her molecular equipment to launch it right back at Senya and Fedya, sparking a full kitchen prank war.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Там сложная формула.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У нас появился ещё один шутник.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочешь войны? Будет тебе торпеда в борт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Сеня, ну не надо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Алло, как дела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Особо ничего.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты когда прилетаешь? Завтра?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Мне тут Нагиев сюрприз сделал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ну, приедешь, расскажу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Лена, я перезвоню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Быстро прекратили!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Работать всем!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Там сложная формула.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There is a complex formula there.' Fedya brings out the contrast between 'there' and 'complex' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У нас появился ещё один шутник.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have got ourselves another prankster.' Fedya brings out the contrast between 'at' and 'us' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочешь войны? Будет тебе торпеда в борт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you want war? You will get a torpedo to the hull.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'want' and finishing on 'board'. Senya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Сеня, ну не надо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Senya, come on, don't.' The meaning hinges on 'Senya' working together with 'need' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Алло, как дела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, how are things?' The grammar packs 'hello' and 'things to do' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Особо ничего.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nothing special.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'particularly' and finishing on 'nothing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты когда прилетаешь? Завтра?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'When do you fly in? Tomorrow?' The grammar packs 'you' and 'tomorrow' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Мне тут Нагиев сюрприз сделал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nagiyev gave me a surprise here.' The meaning hinges on 'to me' working together with 'made' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ну, приедешь, расскажу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, when you arrive, I will tell you.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'come on' and 'arrive' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Лена, я перезвоню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lena, I will call you back.' Chef Barinov anchors the line on 'Lena', while 'will call back' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Быстро прекратили!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Knock it off right now!' The grammar packs 'quickly' and 'stopped' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Работать всем!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everyone get to work!' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'to work' and finishing on 'to everyone'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
